1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Practical questions often ask about validity, reliability or

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,600
accuracy, and then ask you to improve the

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:07,120
method.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:09,840
In four of the last twelve Chemistry and

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:13,200
Biology marking feedback reports, NESA asked for a

6
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,920
clearer understanding of these three words and the

7
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:17,520
difference between them.

8
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:20,320
And in one recent Chemistry guideline, full marks

9
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,880
needed each change to be justified by what

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:24,880
it does for accuracy.

11
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,960
So let's get the three words straight, and

12
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,680
write fixes that actually fix the problem.

13
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:33,660
Three words, three different questions.

14
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:36,500
Validity: does the method measure what it was

15
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:37,420
meant to?

16
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,740
Reliability: if you repeat it the same way,

17
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:42,260
do the results agree?

18
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:45,300
Accuracy: how close is the result to the

19
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:46,540
true value?

20
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:48,340
Picture a target.

21
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:52,020
Results clustered tightly but off centre are reliable,

22
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:53,540
not accurate.

23
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,900
Results scattered around the centre aren't reliable.

24
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:58,980
And a tight cluster on the wrong target

25
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,780
isn't valid, however neat it looks.

26
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:04,980
Right target, repeats agree, close to true.

27
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:08,800
So every answer needs three things.

28
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,120
The word: which quality is the question about?

29
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:15,320
The feature: which part of this method affects

30
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,400
it, and what does it do to the

31
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:18,240
result?

32
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,400
Then the fix: a change that removes that

33
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:22,240
cause.

34
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,440
Word, feature, fix.

35
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,400
If your fix would work for any experiment,

36
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,640
it probably isn't fixing this one.

37
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:33,426
Here's an HSC-style Chemistry question.

38
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:37,266
A student compares ethanol and propan-one-ol in a

39
00:01:37,347 --> 00:01:40,306
spirit burner under an uncovered copper can, with

40
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:41,506
no draught shield.

41
00:01:42,147 --> 00:01:45,186
Because the propan-one-ol flame was taller, she raised

42
00:01:45,227 --> 00:01:46,786
the can for those trials.

43
00:01:47,107 --> 00:01:49,026
Her three trials and the means are in

44
00:01:49,067 --> 00:01:49,586
the table.

45
00:01:50,147 --> 00:01:53,426
There are three parts: reliability, a claim about

46
00:01:53,507 --> 00:01:55,666
more trials, and validity.

47
00:01:56,067 --> 00:01:57,906
If you take Biology, you don't need the

48
00:01:57,987 --> 00:02:00,706
chemistry here: watch how each answer names the

49
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:03,106
word, the feature and the fix.

50
00:02:03,587 --> 00:02:04,626
Your question is coming.

51
00:02:04,947 --> 00:02:06,226
Pause here to answer.

52
00:02:07,434 --> 00:02:09,593
Part a: reliability.

53
00:02:10,074 --> 00:02:11,193
That's about repeats.

54
00:02:11,754 --> 00:02:14,713
Ethanol's three trials sit within about one percent

55
00:02:14,754 --> 00:02:17,593
of their mean, and propan-one-ol's within about one

56
00:02:17,674 --> 00:02:18,393
and a half.

57
00:02:18,794 --> 00:02:20,473
So her results are reliable.

58
00:02:21,034 --> 00:02:24,073
Better still, use the table: the gap between

59
00:02:24,154 --> 00:02:26,633
the two alcohols, about a hundred kilojoules per

60
00:02:26,714 --> 00:02:29,193
mole, is far bigger than the spread within

61
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:32,233
either set, so the difference isn't just scatter.

62
00:02:33,114 --> 00:02:36,073
A judgement, and evidence from the stimulus.

63
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:40,420
Part b: she says ten trials would bring

64
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,820
her values closer to the data values.

65
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:45,140
That's a claim about accuracy.

66
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:48,020
Her values are well under half the data

67
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:51,540
values, because heat escapes from an uncovered, unshielded

68
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:53,220
can in every trial.

69
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:55,780
More trials just give a better mean of

70
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:57,700
the same low values.

71
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,460
So the claim is wrong: repeats improve reliability,

72
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:03,300
not accuracy.

73
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:04,500
What would help?

74
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:06,900
A draught shield and a lid, because they

75
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:08,500
cut the heat that escapes.

76
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,540
Part c: validity.

77
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,380
To compare the alcohols fairly, only the alcohol

78
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:16,420
should change.

79
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:18,500
But she raised the can from two to

80
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:21,620
six centimetres for propan-one-ol, so more of its

81
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:23,780
heat spread into the air before it reached

82
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,420
the can.

83
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:27,700
Look at the numbers: ethanol caught about forty

84
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:31,140
three percent of its data value, propan-one-ol only

85
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,820
about thirty four.

86
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:34,500
So part of the difference comes from the

87
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:36,580
distance, not the alcohol.

88
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:40,180
The fix: keep the can two centimetres above

89
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:42,500
the wick for both, and trim the taller

90
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:44,820
wick so the flame doesn't touch the can.

91
00:03:46,034 --> 00:03:48,593
Now compare two improvements for part c.

92
00:03:49,074 --> 00:03:52,353
Answer A says: repeat the experiment and be

93
00:03:52,434 --> 00:03:53,233
more careful.

94
00:03:53,714 --> 00:03:56,113
That could go on any practical, and neither

95
00:03:56,194 --> 00:03:57,793
part changes the distance.

96
00:03:58,034 --> 00:04:00,753
Answer B names the change and says what

97
00:04:00,834 --> 00:04:01,793
it fixes.

98
00:04:02,034 --> 00:04:05,233
In one recent Chemistry guideline, two changes each

99
00:04:05,394 --> 00:04:08,033
justified by their effect on accuracy got three

100
00:04:08,114 --> 00:04:11,393
marks, while two changes named without reasons got

101
00:04:11,554 --> 00:04:11,953
two.

102
00:04:12,514 --> 00:04:15,113
So when does repeat it earn marks?

103
00:04:15,314 --> 00:04:18,353
When the question is about reliability: in one

104
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:21,953
Biology guideline, explaining how repeats show whether results

105
00:04:22,034 --> 00:04:23,793
agree earned the marks.

106
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,120
Risk questions work the same way: the hazard,

107
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,360
the harm it causes, and a control that

108
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:32,560
stops it, for this substance.

109
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:35,800
These alcohols are flammable, so refill a burner

110
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:38,200
only when it's cold and away from any

111
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:38,760
flame.

112
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:41,960
Writing just wear a lab coat doesn't say

113
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:43,640
what it protects against.

114
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:46,320
In one Biology guideline, the marks needed the

115
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,240
precaution and how it prevents infection.

116
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:51,106
Your turn.

117
00:04:51,347 --> 00:04:54,026
Do the one in your subject, or both.

118
00:04:54,307 --> 00:04:55,546
Chemistry first.

119
00:04:55,907 --> 00:04:58,386
Two students each measure the molar enthalpy of

120
00:04:58,467 --> 00:05:00,746
neutralisation three times.

121
00:05:01,027 --> 00:05:04,306
The accepted value is about minus fifty seven.

122
00:05:04,387 --> 00:05:06,106
Their results are on screen.

123
00:05:06,387 --> 00:05:09,826
Compare the reliability and accuracy of their results.

124
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:10,866
Three marks.

125
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:12,546
Pause here to answer.

126
00:05:22,967 --> 00:05:26,166
X's results agree within one kilojoule per mole,

127
00:05:26,247 --> 00:05:29,206
and Y's spread over seven, so X is

128
00:05:29,287 --> 00:05:30,286
more reliable.

129
00:05:30,567 --> 00:05:32,966
But Y's values are closer to minus fifty

130
00:05:33,127 --> 00:05:36,086
seven, and its mean is minus fifty seven,

131
00:05:36,167 --> 00:05:38,046
so Y is more accurate.

132
00:05:38,407 --> 00:05:41,166
X is consistently less negative, a sign of

133
00:05:41,207 --> 00:05:43,206
a systematic error like heat loss.

134
00:05:43,847 --> 00:05:46,486
The mix-up to drop: X is more accurate

135
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:48,206
because its results agree.

136
00:05:48,407 --> 00:05:50,246
Agreeing is reliability.

137
00:05:51,467 --> 00:05:52,786
Now Biology.

138
00:05:53,067 --> 00:05:55,386
A student compares the microbes that grow from

139
00:05:55,547 --> 00:05:57,786
tap water and from tank water.

140
00:05:58,107 --> 00:06:00,186
She counts the tap water plate after twenty

141
00:06:00,347 --> 00:06:02,826
four hours, and the tank water plate after

142
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:04,186
seventy two.

143
00:06:04,387 --> 00:06:06,026
Everything else is on screen.

144
00:06:06,347 --> 00:06:09,546
Explain why her conclusion is not valid, and

145
00:06:09,667 --> 00:06:12,266
propose a change that fixes the problem.

146
00:06:12,387 --> 00:06:13,306
Three marks.

147
00:06:13,547 --> 00:06:14,906
Pause here to answer.

148
00:06:25,334 --> 00:06:27,653
The incubation time changed as well as the

149
00:06:27,734 --> 00:06:28,213
water.

150
00:06:28,774 --> 00:06:31,333
Given longer, more colonies grow big enough to

151
00:06:31,534 --> 00:06:33,573
count, so the difference could come from the

152
00:06:33,654 --> 00:06:35,173
time, not the water.

153
00:06:35,814 --> 00:06:38,773
The fix: incubate both plates for the same

154
00:06:38,854 --> 00:06:41,493
time, say forty eight hours, and count them

155
00:06:41,574 --> 00:06:42,293
together.

156
00:06:42,614 --> 00:06:45,733
The habit to drop: repeat with more plates.

157
00:06:45,974 --> 00:06:49,333
That improves reliability, but every repeat still has

158
00:06:49,494 --> 00:06:50,693
unequal times.

159
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:54,580
So, in every practical question.

160
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:58,700
Name the word: right target, repeats agree, close

161
00:06:58,780 --> 00:06:59,540
to true.

162
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:01,820
Point to the feature of this method, and

163
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:03,740
say what it does to the result.

164
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:06,700
Then give a fix that removes that cause.

165
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:09,500
Repeats help reliability, but they can't fix an

166
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:12,540
error that pushes every trial the same way.

